prompt:
Make a 20-second Game of Life video for a generative-art channel, starting from the seed in seed.txt: 24 lines of 40 characters, '#' for a live cell and '.' for a dead one (line 1 is the top row). Everything on screen must come from actually running the rules.

Rules: Conway's B3/S23 on a bounded 40 × 24 grid: cells outside the grid are always dead (no wrap-around) and all cells update at the same time.

Format: 1280x720, 30 fps, exactly 20 s (600 frames). Generation 0 is shown until 1.0 s; after that the grid advances one generation every 0.1 s, so generation g (g ≥ 1) appears at t = 0.9 + 0.1 × g s and the last frames show generation 190.

Grid: square cells of at least 20 px with thin grid lines and the whole 40 × 24 board visible inside a border. Colour each live cell by its age, the number of consecutive generations it has already been alive before the current one: age 0 (just born; every cell of generation 0 counts as age 0) #FDE68A, age 1 to 9 #FB923C, age 10 or more #38BDF8. Dead cells show the background.

Side panel:
- 'Generation N' and 'Population P' (live cells), updated every generation;
- 'Peak P at gen G': the highest population so far and the first generation that reached it;
- a line chart of population against generation (x from 0 to 190, y from 0 to 100 with gridlines every 20) drawn only up to the current generation, with a dot on the latest point;
- once the board has started repeating with period 1 or 2, a badge 'Settled at generation S', where S is the first generation whose state equals the state two generations later. The badge appears on generation S + 2, the first moment this can be seen, and stays;
- a legend for the three age colours.

Title at the top: 'Life from a seed' with the subtitle 'B3/S23 · 40 × 24 · no wrap'. Dark background, Inter, and digits in fixed-width cells so the counters never jitter.
